From: Max More <more@chaph.usc.edu>
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 11:38:19 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-493] Extropianism & novelty

Nick Szabo:
> I'd also like to reiterate, in response to Reilly Jones, that
> that recognizing the consequences of these far future technologies 
> is the core new element in Extropianism.  All the other elements
> of Extropianism, while they may be considered important, are
> borrowed.  For example, philosophy from Nietchze, Bartley, etc.

I agree that recognizing the consequences of far (and not so far) future 
technologies is a new element in Extropianism (at least in terms of the 
sheer breadth of this recognition). However, I doubt it's "the" core new 
element. Dynamic Optimism seems to be a fresh idea. While I've found 
many other optimists in the past, this optimism was often either a blind 
faith in progress (brought about by forces external to self), or didn't 
clearly stress the tight bonding of the ideas of optimism + rationality + 
effort and personal responsibility.

Like any complex and deep perspective or ideology, the Extropian 
combines and integrates many pre-existing ideas. The breadth of its 
integration, its open-endedness, and the novelty of its particular 
combination explain much of its attraction and value for (some kinds of) 
people.

> One could imagine using the core Extropian contribution and applying
> other philosophies, although perhaps Nietchze and Bartley fit
> better.

This could (and probably will) be done. Of course then it wouldn't be 
Extropianism but a variant form of transhumanism.

> Personally, I don't need to buy whole hog into all these other
> elements to be quite inspired by the core Extropian breakthrough,
> so I don't consider them as important to Extropiniasm per se.

To others, some of the other elements of Extropian thought may be just 
as important. For instance, to someone struggling to overcome an 
upbringing immersed in faith, the critical rationalist and Nietzschean 
elements may be crucial -- they may need to be applied before that 
person can free themselves up enough to think clearly about how the 
future can affect their life in the present.

> I do agree that transforming our current lives, in light of those insights
> about the far future, should recieve more emphasis, but we cannot
> abandon exploration of the shape of that far future without 
> abandoning Extropianism.

I have always tried to put plenty of stress on transforming our current 
lives in as many areas as possible, rather than simply sitting back and 
theorizing about the future. I made self-transformation (or self-
transcendence) a core principle of Extropianism. Someone is not much of 
an *Extropian* (though they may be a futurist) if they chatter on about 
the wonderful possibilities of the future while failing to develop and 
expand their talents and knowledge, while neglecting the health and 
strength of their body, and while not bothering to overcome typically 
human personality and relationship weaknesses.

The "all theory, little practice" phenomenon has always bothered me 
about many futurists, including too many cryonicists. I find myself 
bemused by self-professed immortalists who have signed up for cryonic 
suspension yet who smoke cigarettes (above levels the body can handle), 
don't exercise, pay no attention to diet, or show no interest in personal 
safety and financial security.  (At least those who've signed up are doing 
better than immortalists who haven't gotten around to it despite 
agreeing to its desirability and workability.) I would be delighted if 
Extropian influences led to more self-transformation among 
immortalists and futurists.

Onward!

