I have similar experiences from going to Montessori schools. (Yes, right
here in statist, socialist Sweden where good schools are so desperately
needed.)

I think the Montessori-method provided a powerful epistemological
kick-start in my intellectual life. I have discovered my current interest
in Extropian philosophy is probably in part rooted in the implicit (and now
explicit) emphasis on reason I learned then.

I should point out that my parents took very much personal interest in my
early education. This I consider the most important gift one could give a
small child. (My mother taught me to read and write long before I attended
school and I also have early memories of my father pointing out the
different but similar sorts of bumblebees living in our garden among other
things. That was exciting!)

Another thing I come to think of is the importance of _individual_
education for children. By this I mean not just arbitrary methods tailored
for certain individuals, but an emphasis on _individual learning_ as
opposed to learning in groups. I think silent exploration without having to
consider the points of views of others is crucial to gain reliance on ones
own reason.

BTW: Ayn Rand wrote a good essay in her most furious style about the mind
destroying methods practiced in most schools today. I think It's called
"Comprachicos". It's horrifying reading.


