From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 16:53:59 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-500] BASICS:  Environment and markets

Phil's been at it again.  OK, here is The Official Extropian Policy on the
Environment [1]:

 1) Ensure that all resources are privately owned.
 2) Eliminate the government.

Tragedies of the commons (see Garret Hardin's original essay if you're not
familiar with this phenomenon) are caused by unowned resources.  Since the
resource is owned by "everybody", the costs of any particular abuse of that
resource are borne almost exclusively by people other than the abuser; so
everyone with access to that resource is incentivized to gobble it
wantonly.

Owned resources, on the other hand, have a particular guardian with a
strong financial incentive to preserve the value of those resources.
Owners who don't look after their property will be selected out of the
economy (go broke), leaving all resources (ceteris paribus) in the hands of
those who do.

What is the value of a resource?  Whatever use the owner wanted it for, of
course.  There is no absolute value for any resource; the value of
something is whatever price you can get for it or whatever you can use it
for.  Suppose I find a mint-condition Mickey Mantle baseball card in my
attic.  Such a card is worthless to me, so I will likely let the card go
for whatever I can get for it.  If the card were found instead by a
collector, she would refuse to let the card go for less than what's it's
worth to her as a collectible.  The value of a resource depends on who owns
it, who wants it, and what they think they can use it for or sell it for
later.

The usual proponents of the "absolute value" hypothesis are
environmentalists who have the desire but not the wherewithal to bid up the
price of a resource to discourage its use, and thus represent the future
value of the resource (when it will be rare) in today's market.

Let's suppose that the world has a finite supply of the mineral plebiscite.
Right now, the price of plebiscite is low, because a new plebiscite mine
was just dug in the District of Columbia.  However, there will be a
terrible plebiscite shortage 25 or 50 years down the road [2].

Let us further suppose that I am an environmentally-aware financial concern
(say, a large bank, or the Nature Conservancy) big enough to have a 25 to
50 year planning horizon, such that I can afford to sink a big chunk of
money into buying up plebiscite and burying it in tapioca mines, then wait
decades to sell it at a big profit later when it's rare [3].  The price
goes up in response to my buying and represents more the "actual" value (as
judged by the Standard Benchmark Environmentalist).  Great so far, right?

The truth is _no_ financial concern can afford to plan for such a long
range, because no one can predict what the government will do.  Erratic
regulatory interventionism makes long-term planning impossible.  (1936:  "I
just bought 150,000 acres of hemp farms.  I'll be a rope and paper tycoon!"
1937:  "The government just illegalized hemp!  I'm a pauper!")  No one can
tell, over the next 50 or 25 or even ten years, which industry will be
nationalized or subsidized, which products will be banned or
price-supported, which businesses will be seized, taxed, bailed out, or
shut down by the runaway governmental regulatory apparatus.  The government
makes long-term planning impossible, hence all market players are forced to
shorten their planning horizons if they want to survive economically.

Furthermore, the government contributes to tragedy-of-the-commons problems
by making it _easier_ to externalize one's costs (e.g., the S&L bailout,
Superfund cleanups, mining and logging on government lands, etc.).
                                        dV/dt
                                        see also "The Machinery of Freedom"
                                        "The Ultimate Resource," and
                                        "Economics In One Lesson"

[1] What, you didn't know that I'm empowered to speak for all extropians
everywhere?  It's true; just ask me. :-)

[2] Fat chance, but I can dream, can't I?

[3] Never mind that ephemeralization will enable us to get by on one-fourth
as much plebiscite by then.



